 Solid R, with no rater calling it competitive", "Hamadeh holds roughly a six-to-one fundraising advantage over Greene-Placentia", "No public general-election polling; markets price about 85 percent Republican"], "rationale": "The district's eight-point Republican lean, Hamadeh's incumbency and clear money advantage, and a rater consensus spanning Likely to Solid Republican all point to a comfortable incumbent win, with prediction markets concurring at roughly 85 percent Republican. Nothing in the evidence suggests the Democratic challenger has the resources or traction to close that gap.", "watch": "A sharply favorable national environment for Democrats or a Hamadeh misstep gaining traction could narrow the margin, though the seat's lean makes an upset unlikely.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Abraham Hamadeh (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 8th District leans Republican by about eight points, and first-term incumbent Abraham Hamadeh is seeking re-election after securing the nomination. Democrat Bernadette Greene-Placentia, a former trucker who won her primary, faces a steep structural disadvantage in a seat that has consistently elected Republicans. No independent public polls of the general election are available, so the picture rests on district fundamentals and outside assessments.\n\nRaters across the spectrum place the race between Likely and Solid Republican. Hamadeh holds a clear financial edge, having raised nearly six times as much as his challenger, and prediction markets give him about an 85 percent chance of winning. His low-profile style and protests over closed-door events have drawn local attention, but nothing indicates a competitive race. The expected result is a high single-digit Republican win with modest residual uncertainty given the absence of polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -8.56}, {"date":